# RouterOS 7 `/routing ospf neighbor print terse`.
#
# ntc-templates ships two mikrotik_routeros OSPF-neighbour templates and NEITHER
# parses RouterOS 7: both were written against v6, which emits
# `instance= router-id= address= interface= priority=` in that order. v7 dropped
# `interface=`/`priority=` entirely, added `area=`, moved `router-id=` after
# `address=`, and prefixes each row with the flag column (`D` = dynamic). So the
# ntc templates fail with a TextFSM State Error rather than mis-parsing —
# verified live against CHR 7.16.2.
#
# `terse` matters: the plain `print` wraps one record across several lines at the
# terminal width, which no line-oriented TextFSM template can follow.
Value Required INDEX (\d+)
Value FLAGS (\S*)
Value INSTANCE (\S+)
Value AREA (\S+)
Value ADDRESS (\S+)
Value ROUTER_ID (\S+)
Value STATE (\S+)
Value STATE_CHANGES (\d+)
Value ADJACENCY (\S+)
Value TIMEOUT (\S+)

Start
  ^\s*${INDEX}\s+${FLAGS}\s+instance=${INSTANCE}\s+area=${AREA}\s+address=${ADDRESS}\s+router-id=${ROUTER_ID}(\s+state=${STATE})?(\s+state-changes=${STATE_CHANGES})?(\s+adjacency=${ADJACENCY})?(\s+timeout=${TIMEOUT})?\s*$$ -> Record
  ^\s*$$
  ^. -> Error
